SECTION 1: TESTING ADMIN ROLE WORKFLOW (POSTMAN + SQL)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
 
Step 1 - Register a normal user
    In Postman, register a user with name, email, and password. This user will be created with the default role: USER.
Step 2 - Manually promote to admin via SQL
    Go to your database (MySQL) and run an UPDATE query to change that user's role from USER to ADMIN.
Step 3 - Login again
    Login with the same user's credentials. The JWT/response should now show role as ADMIN.
Step 4 - Check the dashboard
    Call GET /api/dashboard: This should return income, expense, and balance for the logged-in user.
Step 5 - Create more test users
    Register a few more users normally (they will all get role: USER).
Step 6 - Login as admin
    Login using the admin account created in Step 3.
Step 7 - List all users (admin only)
    Call GET /api/admin/users: This should return the full list of registered users.
Step 8 - Promote a user to admin
    Call PUT /api/admin/users/{user_id}/promote: Replace {user_id} with the actual ID of the user to promote.
Step 9 - Verify the promotion
    Login as the user that was just promoted.Check their role in the response - it should now be ADMIN.
 
 
--------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 2: GOOGLE OAUTH2 LOGIN SETUP
--------------------------------------------------------------------
 
Step 1 - Go to Google Cloud Console
    URL: https://console.cloud.google.com/
Step 2 - Create or select a project
    APIs & Services -> Credentials -> Create Credentials -> OAuth client ID
Step 3 - Configure the OAuth client
    Application type : Web application
    Application name : Expense Tracker
    Authorized redirect URI: http://localhost:8080/login/oauth2/code/google
    Note: This is the callback URL Google redirects to AFTER the user authenticates.
    Spring Security uses this exact path by default - do not change the path structure.
    Do NOT add the frontend URL here (http://localhost:5173/oauth2/callback)
    - that is a separate, frontend-only redirect handled after Spring Security completes its own callback.
    Adding it to the Google Console redirect URI field will not work and is not needed.
Step 4 - Copy the Client ID and Client Secret
    Google will generate: - Client ID - Client Secret
    These identify and authenticate YOUR APPLICATION to Google - not the end user.
    - Client ID  -> tells Google which application is making the request
    - Client Secret -> proves your backend is authorized to use that OAuth application
    - Treat the Client Secret like a password. Store it only in application.properties / .env (not committed to git)
Step 5 - Backend configuration (application.properties)
    Frontend URL (no trailing slash): app.frontend.url=http://localhost:5173
    OAuth2 scopes (leave as-is): spring.security.oauth2.client.registration.google.scope=email,profile
    This tells Google your app wants access to:
    - email   -> the user's email address
    - profile -> the user's name / basic profile info
    No changes needed to this scope line.
 
 
--------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 3: FRONTEND ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
--------------------------------------------------------------------
 
Depends on which frontend tooling you're using:
    Create React App (old, uses process.env): process.env.REACT_APP_API_URL
    Vite (current project, uses import.meta.env): import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL
    Since your frontend is React + Vite, use: import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL
    Make sure the variable is defined in your .env file as: VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:8080

